  21: ## A. THE ROUND IN FLIGHT
  22: 
  23: ### A1. `#513 sgirekills` -- fix applied and measured, NOT YET COMMITTED
  24: 
  25:     witness       PREFIX_RED_20260908.out   30/44  committed build, re-pinned oracles
  26:                   POSTFIX_20260908.out      44/44  same script after the fix
  27:     kill table    killtable_20260908/       12 mutants, scratch clone, frozen instrument
  28:     trees         part 1 CLEAN on all four; build/ PART2_CLEAR; **build-asan/ PART2 STALE**
  29: 
  30: Five read arms became ACCEPT; `CRIME_RE_TEX` became a latched decline on its own bit.
  31: 
  32: **WHAT I WANT ADJUDICATED:** the TEX asymmetry.  Five siblings accept and one declines, and the
  33: warrant is `sgitexindex` -- the TEX write index is a missing factor of four, only 31 of 112 cells
  34: are reachable, `re_tex` has NO consumer, and `sgitexindex` carries a do-not-code ruling.  **Is
  35: declining the read the right disposition, or does it entrench an asymmetry a later round must
  36: undo?**  The row's own `hold_question` puts the counter-case: a MIRRORED accept would round-trip
  37: per frame while shipping hardware-wrong reads at overlapping offsets.
  38: 
  39: **MEASURED, and it is the reason I am not treating the design as settled by me:** the design panel
  40: REFUSED my first row set with two floor-shaped holes, and the kill table then measured both:
  41: 
  42:     M4_readshift  43/44   read-side index re-map -- caught ONLY by the window's LAST-SLOT row
  43:     M7_farbound   43/44   far-bound shrink      -- caught ONLY by the last-slot row
  44:     M8_texaccept  40/44   TEX -> ACCEPT         -- caught by all four store-first TEX rows
  45: 
  46: **Under my original base-only design, M4 and M7 would both have swept clean.**
  47: 
  48: ---
  49: 
  50: ### A2. *** THE `#513` REPAIR CHAIN -- THREE PASSES, AND THE THIRD FOUND THAT MY REPAIR
  51: ### CLOSED ONE CELL OF FIVE.  This is the item most worth an adjudication. ***
  52: 
  53:     pass 2   6 seats, ALL answered.  codex named ONE latch-collision cell (bit 8) with a
  54:              predicted 44/44 sweep and its mechanism.  Repaired: 2 same-instance rows, both
  55:              orderings.  MEASURED: BASE 46/46 clean, M13 44/46 KILLED.
  56:     pass 3   6 fired, 5 answered (codex REFUSED -- see D3).  FOUR of five said DO NOT CLOSE.
  57:              The repair closed bit 8 and left bits 6, 7, 9, 10 -- the SAME one-token operator
  58:              on the SAME edited line.  kimi: "L2 stands, unrepaired."
  59:     closure  tex xbit widened from bits 0-5 to bits 0-8 -- ONE row, not four.
  60:              MEASURED: BASE_pass3 46/46; M_bit6 45/46 KILLED; M_bit7 45/46 KILLED.
  61:              Bits 9, 10 DISCLOSED (set only from the drawing-engine path) and MEASURED anyway.
  62: 
  63: **THREE THINGS I GOT WRONG, all recorded in the row:**
  64: 
  65: 1. **My `#392` rebuttal was a strawman.**  I argued that rowing latch pairs is unbounded; kimi
  66:    showed the cover is **LINEAR** -- the edited latch against each other used bit -- not quadratic.
  67:    I had imagined all pairs; the closure needs only the edited one.
  68: 2. *** I REPRODUCED THE `#499` SHAPE WHILE CLAIMING TO AVOID IT. ***  I rowed both ORDERINGS of one
  69:    colliding bit and called that the symmetric-cell repair.  The symmetry that mattered was across
  70:    BITS, not across orderings.
  71: 3. **THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, FOURTH INSTANCE, AND THE COST WAS A FULL PASS.**  grok's PASS-2
  72:    answer had already named bits 6, 8, 9 AND 10.  codex named only bit 8.  I integrated the
  73:    narrower answer and shipped it.  **I had the file open** -- I quoted grok's resolution of glm's
  74:    objection from twenty lines above the passage I missed.  *When two seats answer one question,
  75:    DIFF them.*
  76: 
  77: **WHAT I WANT ADJUDICATED:**
  78: * **Is the bits 9/10 DISCLOSURE acceptable, or a breach?**  They are set only from the
  79:   drawing-engine path, which a TEX-read session never enters, so the collision needs a guest that
  80:   BOTH draws AND reads TEX.  The class-closure amendment permits writing into the contract "which
  81:   cells are covered and which are deliberately not" -- but kimi argued an in-closure cell on the
  82:   edited line is a breach regardless.  **These two rules point opposite ways and I have applied
  83:   the amendment; confirm or overrule.**
  84: * **Does the chain close at pass 4?**  The amendment says the closing pass VERIFIES THE ENUMERATION
  85:   rather than hunting cells, which terminates.  Pass 4 is owed and not yet fired.
  86: 
  87: ## B. CRITICAL ROWS ADVANCED TONIGHT -- ready for a disposition
  88: 
  89: ### B1. `alphatbinegsel` -- *** I RAISED THIS med -> high AND THE RANKING IS MY JUDGMENT ***
  90: 
  91: **The defect is source-confirmed by two seats independently, with citations I re-verified myself:**
  92: `ALPHA_TBIA()` -- the OS primitive meaning *invalidate every TLB entry* -- is
  93: `alpha_pal_tbi(-2, 0)`.  `a0` is `uint64_t`, the dispatch is `if (a0 >= 1)`, so `-2` compares as
  94: `0xff..fe` and takes the **VADDR** branch with `a1 = 0`.  **It therefore invalidates cached virtual
  95: page ZERO and nothing else.**  Every other mapping survives a flush the guest OS believes succeeded,
  96: and the Alpha fast path keeps consuming the surviving `host_load`/`host_store` pointers.
  97: 
  98:     verified by me:  ALPHA_TBIA macro passes a1=0; the a0 >= 1 dispatch; vaddr_mask defaults to
  99:                      (int64_t)-1 on 64-bit; the alpha binding site
 100:     repair           if (a0 >= 1 && a0 <= 3)   -- COARSE: it does not give TBIAP its ASM=0 semantics
 101:     witness          DESIGNED by grok, NOT BUILT
 102: 
 103: **QUESTION 1 (severity):** the facts are the seats'; the med -> high raise is mine.  A documented
 104: *invalidate everything* primitive clearing one page, silently, with a guest reaching stale physical
 105: backing -- I put that in the same class as the other HIGH rows.  **Confirm or correct.**
 106: 
 107: **QUESTION 2 (ordering):** codex ranked `alphaswpctxreg` FIRST and labelled that *"a confidence
 108: judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential severity"* -- explicitly because this row's
 109: consequences were then unverified.  **They are now verified, so the input to that ranking has
 110: changed.  Should this row go first instead?**
 111: 
 112: **QUESTION 3 (claim scope):** the repair is coarse.  codex supplied the accurate CHANGELOG sentence
 113: and its limit.  **Is shipping the coarse repair right, or does the round owe ASM=0 handling?**
 114: 
 115: ### B2. `alphaswpctxreg` -- the easiest resolvable HIGH-adjacent item in the queue
 116: 
 117: One character.  `cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0] = ctx` should be `ALPHA_V0`; the comment directly above
 118: it already says v0.
 119: 
 120:     idiom      grep -n 'ALPHA_V0] =' src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c  ->  EIGHT sites on 2026-09-08.
 121:                swpctx is the LONE OUTLIER among nine return-value sites in its own file.
 122:     oracle     v0_after == ctx_before, printable by the debugger `reg` command -- so guest code
 123:                need not consume the return value
 124:     vacuity    requires pre-call v0 != outgoing ctx, or a stale v0 looks correct
 125: 
 126: **codex refuted my premise:** I claimed this both loses the return value AND clobbers `a0`.  The
 127: clobber is NOT a defect -- in the wrapper `a0` is an output tied to an input, so its incoming value
 128: need not survive.  One defect, two register-state effects.
 129: 
 130: **QUESTION:** anything against just doing this one?  It has a design, an oracle, a vacuity
 131: condition, eight precedents, and no in-tree dependency.
 132: 
 133: ### B3. `sgirandomcolour` (HIGH, open, never held) -- design pass 1 complete, 5 seats
 134: 
 135: Twelve of sixteen encodable colour modes reach a `default:` arm that fills a buffer with `random()`
 136: and writes it to guest memory; one of the twelve (`YCRCB 0xf0`) is documented in `crmfbreg.h`.
 137: 
 138:     disposition   member 3 (decline + latched announce) via candidate (b) -- skip the PUT's
 139:                   memory_rw.  NEVER an early return.
 140:     scope         TWO rounds, not one with sgirebufuninit.  5 of 5 seats.
 141:     settled by me there is NO mode validation: the store is unconditional and unmasked, the
 142:                   MODE_DST case only debug()s, and the file says "Treat all registers as
 143:                   read/write, by default".  **So the witness IS constructible.**
 144:     settled by me the function has NO common tail, and fatal() does not exit
 145: 
 146: **grok reframed it better than my brief did:** the PUT's output is the STORE, not `buf`, so this is
 147: not a fill policy at all.  And it declined to certify its own recommendation until the tail was
 148: checked -- I checked it.
 149: 
 150: **A DETECTOR HOLE, and three of five seats were in it:** pinning the test to `0xf0` alone is
 151: escapable by `case 0xf0: put_ok = 0; break;` with `default:` still random.  The primary vector must
 152: be an UNDOCUMENTED value.
 153: 
 154: **QUESTION:** this is the highest-severity row I can start immediately after `#513`.  **Does it go
 155: next, or does `alphatbinegsel` / `alphaswpctxreg` outrank it?**
 156: 
 157: ### B4. `armbfcdecode` -- *** BLOCKED ON A DOCUMENT, and this is the one thing I cannot resolve ***
 158: 
 159: codex verified the implementation half: the decoder does not exclude source field 15, so BFC is
 160: dispatched as BFI reading `r[15]`.  **And it corrected me twice** -- `r[15]` is NOT the PC in this
 161: interpreter (translation reconstructs into `cpu->pc`, PC+8 lives in a separate `tmp_pc`), and
 162: DDI 0100I covers ARMv6, not just ARMv5.
 163: 
 164: **BFI/BFC appear in NONE of the fifteen manuals in `_scratchpad/`.**  codex explicitly refused to
 165: cite the architecture from memory -- correctly, given `#357`/`#362`/`#365` shipped page citations
 166: that came from model output.
 167: 
 168: **QUESTION FOR THE OWNER, not for Fable: fetch DDI 0406 (ARMv7-A ARM)?**  Until then this row
 169: cannot ship a behavioural change.
