regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxreg/codex_fixdesign_20260908.txt
   1: **Verdict: return the old context pointer in `ALPHA_V0` (`$0`). The one-character edit is correct. There is one demonstrated ABI defect: this wrapper already permits `a0` to be clobbered.**
   2: 
   3: **Q1.** The full [in-tree wrapper](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/include/thirdparty/alpha_cpu.h:448) is:
   4: 
   5: ```c
   6: static __inline unsigned long
   7: alpha_pal_swpctx(unsigned long ctx)
   8: {
   9: 	register unsigned long a0 __asm("$16") = ctx;
  10: 	register unsigned long v0 __asm("$0");
  11: 
  12: 	__asm volatile("call_pal %2 # PAL_OSF1_swpctx"
  13: 		: "=r" (a0), "=r" (v0)
  14: 		: "i" (PAL_OSF1_swpctx), "0" (a0)
  15: 		/* clobbers t0, t8..t11, a0 (above) */
  16: 		: "$1", "$22", "$23", "$24", "$25", "memory");
  17: 
  18: 	return (v0);
  19: }
  20: ```
  21: 
  22: `=` declares an output; `r` requests a register. The explicit register declarations determine which physical registers these operands use. A numeric input constraint matches an output operand�?Ts location. These are GCC�?Ts [constraint rules](https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc-12.5.0/gcc/Extended-Asm.html#InputOperands) and [local-register binding rules](https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc-8.4.0/gcc/Local-Register-Variables.html).
  23: 
  24: | Operand | Constraint/expression | Location and role |
  25: |---|---|---|
  26: | 0 | `"=r" (a0)` | `$16`: output whose resulting value is discarded |
  27: | 1 | `"=r" (v0)` | `$0`: output returned by `return (v0)` |
  28: | 2 | `"i" (PAL_OSF1_swpctx)` | Immediate PAL number substituted for `%2` |
  29: | 3 | `"0" (a0)` | Input tied to **operand 0**, hence `$16`, carrying `ctx` |
  30: 
  31: Thus `"0"` refers to **output operand zero**, which here occupies **register sixteen**. It neither selects physical `$0` nor transfers the result into it. The emulator�?Ts [register definitions](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/include/cpu_alpha.h:75) agree: `ALPHA_V0 = 0`, `ALPHA_A0 = 16`.
  32: 
  33: **Q2.** One defect with two register-state effects. The existing assignment leaves `v0` without the required result and overwrites `a0`. But that overwrite is **permitted by the wrapper�?Ts constraints**: `a0` is an output tied to an input, so its incoming value need not survive.
  34: 
  35: The smallest edit at [line 251](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c:251) is:
  36: 
  37: ```diff
  38: -		cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;
  39: +		cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_V0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;
  40: ```
  41: 
  42: This supplies the return value and removes that `a0` overwrite. No separate preservation handling or wrapper change is needed. Preserving `a0` becomes this implementation�?Ts behavior, without becoming an ABI requirement.
  43: 
  44: **Q3.** Yes. The closest active sibling is [the `swpipl` case](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c:337):
  45: 
  46: ```c
  47: 	case 0x35:	/*  PAL_OSF1_swpipl  */
  48: 		/*  a0 = new ipl, v0 = return old ipl  */
  49: 		cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_V0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ps & ALPHA_PSL_IPL_MASK;
  50: 		cpu->cd.alpha.ps &= ~ALPHA_PSL_IPL_MASK;
  51: 		cpu->cd.alpha.ps |= (a0 & ALPHA_PSL_IPL_MASK);
  52: 		break;
  53: ```
  54: 
  55: It writes the old state directly into `ALPHA_V0`, then installs the new state from the copied `a0`. Other active PAL cases use the same destination: `rdmces` returns `mces` at line 204, `rdps` returns `ps` at 344, and `whami` returns `cpu_id` at 361.
  56: 
  57: The earlier return at [line 118](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c:118) belongs to `PROM_R_PUTS` in `alpha_prom_call()`, which also writes `ALPHA_V0`.
  58: 
  59: **Q4.** The host local `a0` remains live through [`cpu->cd.alpha.ctx = a0`](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c:255). Your premise about that copy is correct.
  60: 
  61: The rest of this case does not consume `r[ALPHA_A0]` as an old-context result. Its [direct emulator caller](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_instr.c:63) checks execution status and PC afterward; it does not read `a0` or move it into `v0`. The wrapper discards its `a0` output, and the searched `src/` contains no calls to that wrapper.
  62: 
  63: No in-tree dependency was found. An external guest explicitly relying on the accidental old-context value in `a0` would observe a change; the inspected wrapper provides no such guarantee.
  64: 
  65: **Q5.** Let `C` be the outgoing context pointer, `N` the incoming argument, and `P` the pre-call `v0`. On normal completion, before subsequent instructions overwrite these registers:
  66: 
  67: | Observable | Existing code | Fixed code |
  68: |---|---|---|
  69: | `v0` | `P` | `C` |
  70: | `a0` | `C` | `N` |
  71: 
  72: The return-value oracle is **`v0_after == ctx_before`**. A distinguishing observation requires `P != C`; otherwise stale `v0` happens to look correct.
  73: 
  74: The debugger�?Ts [`reg` command](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/debugger/debugger_cmds.c:1644) reaches the [Alpha register dump](/C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha.c:170), which prints both registers by name. **Guest code need not consume the return value for the debugger to distinguish the builds.**
  75: 
  76: **Q6.** Keep the comment. �?oReturn old context in v0�?? asserts an observable postcondition: both the returned value and its register. Under your stated project rule, it owes a test row pinning the Q5 oracle. Calling it a description does not remove that claim. It makes no claim about preserving `a0`.
  77: 
  78: This was a source review; no files were changed and no runtime test row was executed.
regress/artifacts/armbfcdecode/codex_decode_20260908.txt
   1: **Verdict: source field 15 reaches `X(bfi)` and supplies the stored `r[15]` value. That implementation claim holds, but the value is not reliably PC or PC+8. The permitted manual does not establish the encoding�?Ts BFC meaning.**
   2: 
   3: Q1: The decoder does **not** distinguish source 15. The instruction�?Ts source field is called `rm` in this generic decoder: [line 4016](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:4016) reads:
   4: 
   5: ```c
   6: rm    = iword & 15;
   7: ```
   8: 
   9: The [decoder arm, lines 4564�?"4570](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:4564), reads:
  10: 
  11: ```c
  12: } else if ((iword & 0x0fe00070) == 0x07c00010) {
  13: 	ic->f = cond_instr(bfi);
  14: 	ic->arg[0] = (size_t)(&cpu->cd.arm.r[rd]);
  15: 	ic->arg[1] = (size_t)(&cpu->cd.arm.r[rm]);
  16: 	int lsb = (iword >> 7) & 31;
  17: 	int msb = (iword >> 16) & 31;
  18: 	ic->arg[2] = (msb << 16) + lsb;
  19: ```
  20: 
  21: The mask leaves bits `[3:0]` unrestricted, and no enclosing guard excludes source 15. Thus **`arg[1]` carries the address of `r[15]`**. All your settled extraction and packing premises are correct.
  22: 
  23: Its contents depend on execution history. Translation reconstructs the instruction address into [`cpu->pc`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:3967), without refreshing `r[15]` there. PC-relative loads compute PC+8 in the separate [`tmp_pc`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr_loadstore.c:577). This BFI arm does not use that storage.
  24: 
  25: For concrete possible contents, a load into PC leaves its loaded target in [`r[15]`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr_loadstore.c:587); exception entry writes the [exception-vector address](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm.c:731). Consequently, BFI can read a previous target or vector rather than its own instruction address.
  26: 
  27: Q2: The [complete handler, lines 1450�?"1467](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c:1450), is:
  28: 
  29: ```c
  30: X(bfi)
  31: {
  32: 	uint32_t x = reg(ic->arg[1]);
  33: 
  34: 	int lsb = (uint8_t)ic->arg[2];
  35: 	int msb = ic->arg[2] >> 16;
  36: 	int width = msb - lsb + 1;
  37: 
  38: 	x <<= lsb;
  39: 
  40: 	uint32_t mask = (1 << width) - 1;
  41: 
  42: 	mask <<= lsb;
  43: 
  44: 	reg(ic->arg[0]) &= ~mask;
  45: 	reg(ic->arg[0]) |= (x & mask);
  46: }
  47: Y(bfi)
  48: ```
  49: 
  50: It reads the register value unconditionally when the handler executes. There is no source-15 clear path. With defined mask arithmetic, it coincidentally produces a clear when the source�?Ts **low `width` bits** are zero. Those are the bits shifted into the destination field; the relevant source bits are not `[msb:lsb]`.
  51: 
  52: Q3: **BFI/BFC are not in this manual.** I found the specified extract at [GXEMUL/_scratchpad/ddi0100i.txt](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/_scratchpad/ddi0100i.txt). Searches found neither mnemonic nor a bit-field insert/clear entry.
  53: 
  54: One premise needs correction: DDI 0100I is not ARMv5-only. Its [preface explicitly includes ARMv6](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/_scratchpad/ddi0100i.txt:402). That does not supply the later BFI/BFC definitions.
  55: 
  56: Therefore, the source behavior above is independently verified, but **source 15 �+' architectural BFC is not document-verified within this reading scope**. The repair and expected result below use that stated later-ISA rule; I cannot attach a local manual page citation to it.
  57: 
  58: Q4: For this operand-selection defect, the smallest local repair is to replace the initial register read with:
  59: 
  60: ```c
  61: uint32_t x = ic->arg[1] == (size_t)(&cpu->cd.arm.r[ARM_PC])
  62:     ? 0 : reg(ic->arg[1]);
  63: ```
  64: 
  65: The existing pointer preserves the source-register identity, so this makes source 15 supply zero while retaining ordinary BFI behavior. The mask defect still needs its separate repair.
  66: 
  67: A distinguishing observation **is constructible**:
  68: 
  69: - Initialize `r0 = 0xffffffff`.
  70: - Execute a successful word load into PC whose target is `0x00001008`. The inspected load path puts that target in `r[15]` and branches there.
  71: - At that target, execute unconditional `BFC r0, #8, #4`: `lsb = 8`, `msb = 11`, width 4.
  72: 
  73: With `r[15] = 0x00001008`, the low four source bits are `8`, and the destination mask is `0x00000f00`.
  74: 
  75: | Behavior | Result in `r0` |
  76: |---|---|
  77: | Clear the field | `0xfffff0ff` |
  78: | Current handler inserts stored `r[15]` bits | `0xfffff8ff` |
  79: 
  80: Merely placing the instruction at address `0x1008` would not establish that register-slot value; the load-to-PC setup matters. Width 4 avoids the sibling mask boundary cases. These results are source-derived and arithmetic-checked, not an emulator run.
  81: 
  82: Q5: **Keep two rounds.** Correcting source-15 semantics leaves mask construction unchanged; correcting mask construction leaves source-15 semantics unchanged. The width-4 observation above isolates this defect, while a width test can use an ordinary source register.
  83: 
  84: Also, �?oundefined for most encodable widths�?? is incorrect for a 32-bit `int`: positive widths **1�?"30** are fine for this expression. Width **31** encounters the signed boundary, and width **32** shifts by the operand�?Ts bit width. That correction does not weaken the case for separate rounds.

