_scratchpad/panel_sgirekills_pass3_20260908/ollama_minimax.txt:5:**DEFECT 1 (REASONED, answers Q1 + Q2).** The repair closes the bit-8-vs-bit-11 cell in both orderings — that part is correct. But `d->warned` spans bits 0–11, and the new rows exercise ONE bit pair (8/11). Bits 6, 7, 9, 10 are swept by nothing. A mutant that moves SGIRE_W_TEX_RD from 1<<11 to **1<<9** (or any non-{8,11} bit in 6–11) leaves the latch broken and the 46-row detector clean. Q1's answer: **YES**, such a mutant exists. Q2's answer: **YES**, this is the #499 shape — closed the named cell, left its kin — in the bit-pair dimension rather than the ordering dimension. The fact that adding a row per bit pair would be the #392 shape (unbounded growth) does not dissolve the gap; it just means the class needs a different fix (parameterised, or a single row that walks pairs). The author's own Q2 framing explicitly raises this and calls it "coverage debt outside the floor" — but the panel's prior finding was *clause-(d) floor breach*, and the closure is a class, not an instance. Whether this is breach or debt depends on whether the floor requires one exercised instance per closure predicate or full predicate behaviour. **The packet does not resolve this**, and the author has not argued it. I read the stricter reading as more faithful to the chain rule's purpose: a repair that closes the cell the panel named and leaves the symmetric cells is the pattern the rule exists to catch.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirekills_pass3_20260908/ollama_minimax.txt-6-
