_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:1:**VERDICT**: Member 3 (DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE), skipping `memory_rw` in the default arm; once the write is skipped, `buf`'s contents are unobservable and the uninitialised-bytes concern collapses. Two rounds, not one with `sgirebufuninit`.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:7:- **Mask arithmetic**: 0xf0 isolates bits 4-7, admitting 0x00, 0x10, …, 0xf0 — 16 values. Four explicit cases (CI/RGB/RGBA/ABGR), so 12 reach `default:`. Of those 12, exactly one (0xf0/YCRCB) is named in `crmfbreg.h`. Arithmetic checks out. READ
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:19:Member **3 (DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE)**, with control flow **(b)** — skip `memory_rw` for the declined mode. Once the write is skipped, `buf`'s post-state is unobservable: no consumer reads it, so "what does the buffer contain" is the wrong question.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:21:The brief's rejection of (c) "leave `buf` untouched" is correct **only under the assumption that `memory_rw` is still called**. If we skip the write, (b) and (c) collapse to the same observable outcome — the difference is whether `buf[4..7]` were ever written by the host, and since they aren't read, they aren't. The reason (c) is worse *as stated* is that the brief is implicitly assuming the write still happens; under that assumption (c) leaks stack, (a) leaks a synthetic colour, only (b) leaks nothing.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:25:## Q2 — exact control flow if (b)
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:49:Skipped statement: the `cpu->memory_rw(...)` call. I deliberately use a **conditional around the existing call**, not an early `return` or `goto` — this is the failure mode the brief warns about ("four readers proposed an early `return`, none looked nine lines down"). A conditional cannot skip a common tail because there is no early exit to skip from.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:51:**Not checkable from the packet**: what the function does *after* the `if (put) / else` block. If there is cleanup, a return-value computation, or a common tail that runs in both arms, the conditional above preserves it; an early-`return` rewrite would not. The brief shows the function up to the `else` for GET and stops. A seat with the tree must confirm there is no post-block work that the decline path must still execute (and there is no `return` value the caller inspects that would change semantics).
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:53:## Q3 — round scope: one or two?
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:55:**Two rounds.** The semantic classes differ:
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:62:A zero-fill at declaration would *prevent both symptoms*, but it does not unify the triggers or the detectors. A guest can trip `sgirebufuninit` (CI + depth 8) without ever entering the `default:` arm, and a guest can trip this round (YCRCB) without leaving `buf[4..7]` uninitialised in any implemented arm. Two independent invariants, two detectors — the project's rule "more findings create more candidate rounds, never bigger rounds" governs. Joining them under one oracle ("no uninitialised byte ever reaches guest memory") would be a *meta-oracle* that erases the distinction between the two failure modes and makes regressions in either indistinguishable in the test suite.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:66:## Q4 — detector and smallest defeating mutant
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_minimax.txt:87:- **Upstream does not validate.** Then a witness is constructible along the same shape as #504's XFER copy: drive a PUT through this helper on `-E sgi -e ip32` with a guest-programmed `mode` whose type field is 0xf0 (YCRCB). Before fix: `fatal()` aborts the run (red). After fix: emulator survives, latch fires once, no write to `tileptr + ofs` (green). Same script both runs; liveness control = the guest continues past the PUT and exits cleanly.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:1:**VERDICT:** Member 3 — DECLINE + latched announce, implemented as candidate (b) with a one-local guard (`ok`) around the single `memory_rw` statement (not an early return), as a round SEPARATE from `sgirebufuninit`; and one premise is load-bearing yet not checkable from this packet: whether `fatal()` returns.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:5:* [READ] The arithmetic in §2 checks out: mask `0xf0` is one nibble = 16 values; the switch shown has 4 case labels + default; 16 − 4 = 12 unhandled, `0xf0` among them. No defect.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:14:**(b) > (a) > (c)** [REASONED throughout]
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:16:* **(b) skip the write** — correct analogue of `#504`. There, "leave it alone" worked because a *deterministic thing already stood* (caller's `color = fg`). Here the pre-existing deterministic thing is **the guest's own memory**, not `buf`. Declining the write lets guest memory stand; nothing fabricated; same disposition as the shipped sibling fix in the same helper (uniform model semantics).
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:19:* **(d) IMPLEMENT YCRCB** — rejected for this round: 11 of the 12 unhandled values are undefined and *cannot* be implemented, so a decline arm is needed regardless; and a correct YCRCB conversion needs a spec not in this packet. Separate candidate round at best.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:26:* **Not visible from packet: everything after the `else` body.** I therefore do not propose any early `return`; the `if (ok)` guard preserves any unseen tail by construction — this is the recorded failure the brief cites, and the guard pattern is the only proposal safe against it. With the write skipped, `buf` is never handed to the host memory subsystem, so its uninitialised state is irrelevant on this path.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:28:## Q3 — two rounds [REASONED]
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:32:## Q4 — detector: three channels, and the mutants [REASONED]
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:35:1. **Sentinel-unchanged in guest memory:** guest PUTs a distinctive sentinel with a valid mode (e.g. RGB), re-PUTs the same pixels with a declined mode (YCRCB), reads back with a valid-mode GET, compares. Post-fix = sentinel intact. Single-run sound; equality with a fabricated/leaked value is a fluke, not a draw (per-byte sentinel for small `bufdepth`).
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_kimi.txt:45:Not checkable from packet: (i) whether any mode validation masks/rejects these values *before* this helper — settling fact: existence of a mode whitelist in the dispatch/register path; (ii) whether `#504`'s witness actually selected a declined mode (the packet never states its mode). If it did *and* that XFER also PUTs, then either `fatal()` returns or that path never PUT with the bad mode — a conditional that would also settle the pivotal premise above. If validation rejects all 12 values upstream, the round is documented-only — but the `#504` precedent makes that unlikely.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:5:* **Mask arithmetic — confirmed (REASONED).** Mask `0xf0` covers 4 bits → 16 masked values {0x00, 0x10, …, 0xF0}; the snippet shows 4 case labels; 16 − 4 = 12 reach `default:`; 0xf0 is one of the 12 and is the fifth documented type. "12, one documented" is correct.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:6:* **The grep premise is weaker than stated (READ + REASONED).** `grep -c YCRCB == 0` proves token-absence, not unhandled-ness — a numeric `case 0xf0:` would evade it. The inlined switch independently shows no 0xf0 case, so the conclusion holds *for this helper*; "handled nowhere else in the file" rests on the grep alone.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:13:**(a), instantiated arm-locally, ranks first; (b) second; (d) third; (c) last — the brief's rejection of (c) is right.**
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:15:* **(a), arm-local memset** (call it a2): post-fix, `buf[0..7]` are zero and the tile receives `bufdepth` zero bytes. Deterministic, no host-stack leak, no new control flow, and — critically — it does **not** zero-init at declaration, so `sgirebufuninit` stays red for its own round (see Q3). The brief's objection — "publishes a colour the guest never asked for" — is already adjudicated by precedent: #504's shipped fix published `fg`, a colour the guest's GET never asked for either, and the project recorded that as the improvement over noise (READ, quoted). A put engine that always writes `bufdepth` defined bytes is also the smaller semantic invention: a silent no-op put leaves a guest that "cleared" a region with stale pixels and no signal but a latch.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:16:* **(b)** is the defensible runner-up: "the destination's prior content stands" is the strict structural analogue of #504's "the caller's prior stands." But it requires new control flow next to a hazard the project has already been burned by (Q2), and #504's shipped outcome was "the effect still occurs, with a defined substitute," not "the effect is suppressed."
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:22:## Q2 — control flow if (b)
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:24:Skipped statement: exactly one — the `cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS | PHYSICAL);` call. Mechanism: `int do_write = 1;` before the switch; default arm sets `do_write = 0;` and breaks; after the switch, `if (do_write) cpu->memory_rw(...)`. Afterwards control falls to the end of the `if (put)` block; the GET `else` is dead (put is true); then the function's tail. **The tail is not in the packet — I cannot certify an early `return`, and I say so rather than guess.** The flag-guard is the only form certifiable from here precisely because it provably removes one statement's execution and nothing else, making the unseen tail's content irrelevant. This is the direct answer to the four-readers failure.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:26:## Q3 — two rounds
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:30:## Q4 — detector and mutants
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:32:Channel: guest-visible destination bytes (the channel #504's witness already used — READ that it exists; its mechanism is not in the packet) plus exit status, with this script: (1) pre-seed the target bytes **nonzero** via a defined-mode put (this doubles as the before-liveness control); (2) declined put, color-type 0x40, `*color ≠ 0`, at one offset; repeat at a second offset with color-type 0xf0; (3) read back; (4) assert targets all-zero, adjacent bytes unchanged (the write is only `bufdepth` bytes at `ofs`), normal exit; (5) run twice, assert identical transcripts. Green is unambiguous under either resolution of the `fatal()` premise.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_glm.txt:38:Likely constructible in #504's shape (`-E sgi -e ip32`, real dispatch, same script red/green): pre-seed, declined put, read back, post-op defined-mode control proving the device isn't wedged. The packet names `draw_primitive()` as a caller that writes to the destination (READ), so a PUT through this helper is plausibly driven the same way. The single blocking premise is UNSETTLED #2: if the register-write path masks mode bits to defined types or rejects the store upstream, **no witness exists**, the round is documented-only, and "12 of 16" then describes host-reachable states no real guest can reach. The readback channel's mechanism is likewise not shown; I rely on #504's witness having observed destination bytes, which the packet asserts.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:1:VERDICT LINE FIRST: I would pick **(b) skip the `memory_rw` entirely for a declined mode, with a latched announce**, and treat `sgirebufuninit` as a **separate round**.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:6:- **REASONED** and correct: the mask `0x000000f0` admits 16 values; four are implemented; 12 reach the `default:` arm, one of which is documented `YCRCB 0xf0`. Arithmetic checks out.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:8:- **NOT CHECKABLE from packet**: whether there is a common tail after the `if (put) ... else ...` that must run. The proposed control flow below avoids early return, so any such tail would still execute.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:14:1. **(b) Skip the `memory_rw` entirely for a declined mode** — this is the correct DECLINE + latched announce. The buffer contents become irrelevant because they are never consumed. This matches the `#504` precedent: decline means no guest-visible effect.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:21:## Q2 — Exact control flow for (b)
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:46:The `if (put)` branch then ends normally; the `else` GET branch is unaffected. No early return, so any common tail after the `if/else` still runs. I cannot see that tail from the packet, but this structure does not skip it.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:48:## Q3 — Round scope: one round or two?
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:50:**Two rounds.**
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:55:A zero-fill at declaration would mask `sgirebufuninit` but would not correctly fix the declined-mode row — it would still write zeros for a mode the guest never asked for. The two have different oracles: one checks “no write for declined mode”, the other checks “no uninitialised bytes for supported mode with large `bufdepth`”. So they are separate rounds.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:57:## Q4 — Detector and smallest defeating mutant
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:59:**Detector:** a witness script selects an unsupported mode, e.g. `YCRCB 0xf0`, with `bufdepth` 4, performs a PUT to a known tile, then reads the tile back.  
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:69:Likely **yes**, with one caveat. The packet states the guest chooses `color_mode` and that `YCRCB` lands on the `default:` arm, implying no earlier rejection. A witness can be shaped like `#504`’s but on the PUT path: select `YCRCB 0xf0`, perform a PUT, and observe that the destination tile is unchanged after the fix.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\ollama_deepseek.txt:71:The single fact that would settle it: **does the device’s mode-register write path accept `0xf0` and pass it through to `horrible_getputpixel`, or does it mask/reject it earlier?** From the packet alone I cannot see that earlier validation, but the stated premise says `YCRCB` is handled nowhere, which suggests it reaches the `default:` arm.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:9:**Mask arithmetic holds.** `DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK = 0x000000f0` is bits 4–7. `color_mode = mode & that` therefore has exactly the 16 values `0x00, 0x10, … 0xf0`. Four `case`s are shown. The other 12 fall through `default:` in *this* switch. That count is just `16 - 4`; it does not need the header.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:11:**What that does *not* prove:** that 12 values are guest-reachable, or that a documented `YCRCB` is a mode this helper is required to paint. Those are claims about other code. Not in the packet.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:13:**`YCRCB` → `default:` in this file** is given as `grep -c YCRCB = 0`. I cannot re-run that. *If* that count is right, `0xf0` is one of the 12 here. A header enum is not a packing spec; it does not make `IMPLEMENT` the member for this round.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:15:**`sgirebufuninit` as a separate row** is a real, distinct fact from the code you quoted: no arm writes `buf[4..7]`, `CI` writes only `buf[0]`, `bufdepth` is guest-chosen in `{1,2,4,8}`. I am not re-finding it. I will not smuggle its fix into this round either (Q3).
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:25:## Q1. Member 3, candidate (b). `buf` is not the thing you leave alone.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:29:1. **(b) skip that `memory_rw`.** Destination of a PUT is guest memory at `tileptr + ofs`, not `buf`. `#504` declined the helper’s *output*. The PUT output is the store. Skipping the store leaves whatever the guest already had — deterministic, no invented colour, no host bytes. `buf` can stay uninitialised because it is not sent. That is not (c).
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:30:2. **(a) zero `buf[0..bufdepth)` (all of it, not four bytes) and still store.** Deterministic, no control-flow risk, no leak. It *does* publish a colour the guest did not ask for (black / zero). That is the same class of lie as `random()`, with the entropy stripped. Runner-up if and only if a function tail you have not shown consumes `buf` or assumes the store happened.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:32:4. **(c) leave `buf` untouched and still store.** You are not wrong. It is strictly worse: host stack into guest RAM. `#504` is not a defence of this. The GET output was the caller’s `uint32_t color = fg`. `buf` is not that. If someone zero-initialises `buf` at declaration, (c) *becomes* (a); that is a different edit (and it is the Q3 trap).
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:40:- **7 IMPLEMENT** — no YCbCr packing, subsample, or byte order in this packet; 11 of the 12 values are not even named. Do not special-case `0xf0` in the same round as declining the rest (two designs).
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:59:**Skip that one `memory_rw` call, and only when this PUT `switch` took `default:`.** Do not `return`. Do not `break` out of the `if (put)` by returning from the function. The recorded failure mode is exactly an early `return` that skips a common tail.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:82:**That tail is not in the packet.** I will not guess `x++`, a dirty bit, a return value, or a use of `buf`. I cannot certify (b) against “nine lines down.” If an implementer finds a tail that requires the store or reads `buf`, stop; the fallback is (a) (`memset(buf, 0, sizeof buf)` in `default:` only, then the existing `memory_rw`), still member 3, still no `return`.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:88:## Q3. Two rounds. Not one.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:95:| Happens on | the 12 `default:` values | *implemented* arms too (`CI` @ 2/4/8, all arms @ 8) |
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:97:| Fix if (b) | skip the store; `buf` unused | does not touch `CI`/RGB/… |
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:101:**One fix design?** `uint8_t buf[8] = {0};` plus deleting `random()` is two behaviours glued by a C initializer. It would make (b) impossible to express cleanly (you would still store zeros unless you also skip). The standing rule you quoted forbids growing a round because a nearby initializer is convenient.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:105:`(b)` keeps the rows from colliding: default no longer sends `buf`, so this arm stops being an uninit witness, and `CI` with `bufdepth>1` remains for the other round.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:113:## Q4. Detector: dest canary, not “looks non-random.”
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:121:**Do not pin the test to `0xf0` alone.** A mutant `case 0xf0: put_ok = 0; break;` with `default:` still random would escape. Use at least one *undocumented* value (`0x40` is the first hole) as the primary; `0xf0` can be a second vector, not the only one. Vary `bufdepth` at least `{1,4,8}` so a mutant that skips only when `bufdepth==4` dies.
_scratchpad/panel_sgirandomcolour_20260908\grok.txt:127:- **Actual escape of a dest-only oracle:** keep `buf[i] = random()`, set `put_ok = 0` (skip the store). Canary intact, PRNG still runs. Guest-visible defect is gone; leftover `random()` is dead code. I am willing to call that a successful (b), not an escape, **provided the patch also deletes the four `random()` calls** (same edit, not a second oracle). If a later seat wants “`random()` is not called” as a host-side check, that is extra; it is also defeated by `rand()` / an LCG, so I would not make it the oracle.
