ts, so this costs nothing -- and grok (`--prompt-file`) and agy (`--add-dir`) were already immune, leaving these two as the only argv-fed seats. **The block previously WARNED and then launched anyway**, which cost both seats on a 38 KB brief and is the check-that-cannot-fail family's sibling: *a message reporting an ACTION the code does not take*, exactly like the re-firing message. `PANEL_ARGV_CAP` overrides the cap **only so the branch is testable in both directions** without keeping a 38 KB fixture; it is not a tuning knob. Controlled by extracting the real decision block out of `panel.sh` and `eval`ing it -- 6/6 rc=0 fixed, EXTRACT FAILED rc=9 pre-fix. **A HAND-WRITTEN BRIEF STILL NEEDS TO BE READABLE**: the pointer form only helps seats that can open the file, so it is not licence to grow briefs without limit -- the packet-fed Ollama seats still get the whole thing POSTed and still burn budget on it. Never the trailing-`-` stdin form (died silently once). |
..\CLAUDE.md:783:| agy 3.7 (high) | `agy --dangerously-skip-permissions --add-dir <dir> --print-timeout 30m --model gemini-3.7-flash-high --effort high --print "<short prompt pointing at brief file>"` — reads files via `--add-dir`; no stdin prompt mode. **Model set by user directive 2026-08-13.** PASS BOTH `--model` and `--effort`: the effort tier is baked into the model name (`-high`/`-medium`/`-low`) AND there is a separate `--effort` flag, so naming only one leaves the other at its default. Before this, `panel.sh` passed NEITHER while printing "agy(high)" — the banner was an overclaim. **`agy models` is the authority on valid names** (it lists gemini-3.7/3.6/3.5-flash and 3.1-pro tiers, plus claude-sonnet-4-6 and claude-opus-4-6-thinking); a wrong name is a SEAT FAILURE, so re-check after any CLI upgrade.  *** AND IT HAS A QUOTA.  MEASURED 2026-08-30, THE FIRST agy WALL ON RECORD, AND IT ARRIVED WHILE THE SEAT WAS BEING DELIBERATELY PUSHED. ***  `Error: Individual quota reached. Please upgrade your subscription to increase your limits. Resets in 1h56m37s.` -- **111 bytes, on stderr, with the process exiting immediately**, so the `.out` file is ZERO BYTES and nothing is written to the panel dir but that one line.  **THE ROSTER ASSUMPTION IT OVERTURNS:** the owner's own read was *"seems like there is limitless agy use"*, and this project had never seen it wall, so it was being farmed 3 jobs at a time.  Six substantial invocations inside about forty minutes reached the limit.  Treat it like Grok's paid cap: **not queryable, not unlimited, and a raised cap has the same failure mode further out.**  *** THE DETECTION IS EASY AND THE MISREADING IS NOT. ***  It states its own reset time, which no other seat here does -- so a walled agy tells you exactly when to re-fire, and the re-fire-a-walled-seat-once rule becomes a scheduled thing rather than a guess.  But **the zero-byte `.out` is the trap**: this file's own rule says a zero-byte agy log is the NORMAL mid-run state because agy does not stream.  Zero bytes plus a live process is mid-run; zero bytes plus 111 bytes on stderr is a WALL.  **Check the `.err`, not the size** -- `panel.sh` writes one per seat and this is what it is for.  *Recorded because a seat that walls with the machine otherwise healthy is the state this project most often misfiles: three jobs failed identically and instantly, which reads like a launcher bug and is not.* |
